{
  "requirement": "In Markdown, escaped roles and role-looking text in inline code, fenced code, or raw HTML MUST remain literal and MUST NOT count as references. A `{req}` directive inside a fenced code example and a legacy inline `{req}` role MUST NOT define a requirement.",
  "determination": false,
  "rationale": "The implementation covers escaped roles, inline code, fenced code and raw HTML, but I found no test for raw HTML. Raw HTML is one of the three named literal contexts, so the tests don't cover the whole requirement.\n\n**What is implemented and marked**\n- `protected_ranges` (src/lib.rs:730) collects code-block ranges, inline-code ranges, and `Event::Html` and `Event::InlineHtml` ranges from pulldown-cmark.\n- `reference_roles` (src/lib.rs:670) skips any `{` that `is_escaped` (an odd run of backslashes) or `is_protected`.\n- `definition_blocks` (src/lib.rs:508) uses `overlaps_protected` to ignore `:::{req}` fences that fall inside fenced code.\n- Only colon-fence directives define requirements, so a legacy inline `{req}` role never defines one.\n- The {impl} markers on these functions are present and appropriate.\n\n**What is tested**\n- Inline code, fenced code and a backslash-escaped role: `markdown_examples_and_escaped_references_stay_literal` (src/lib.rs:1056) asserts all three marker occurrences stay literal and no reference badge is rendered.\n- A `:::{req}` inside a fenced code block and a legacy `{req}` role: `code_examples_and_legacy_roles_remain_literal` (src/lib.rs:972) and `colon_directives_render_markdown_bodies_and_legacy_roles_stay_literal` (src/lib.rs:1081). The same fixtures check that a genuine definition is still rendered.\n- Fenced code inside a requirement body: `references_in_requirement_bodies_are_scanned_as_markdown` (src/lib.rs:1096).\n- The validator path: `source_references_are_covered_by_evidence_inputs` (src/evidence.rs:1610) shows that inline and fenced examples in a Markdown file are not collected as references.\n\n**Gaps**\n1. No test puts a role inside raw HTML, either a block such as `<div>…</div>` or an HTML comment, or inline HTML. I searched src and tests for HTML fixtures and found none relevant. The raw-HTML branch of `protected_ranges` (`Event::Html` and `Event::InlineHtml`) is therefore unverified.\n2. For inline HTML, pulldown-cmark's `InlineHtml` range covers only the tag itself. In `<span>{impl}`x`</span>` the role text sits between the tags and is not protected, so it would count as a reference. Whether that falls under \"raw HTML\" is ambiguous, and no test pins the intended behaviour.\n\nNone of these gaps carries a FIXME or other acknowledgement in the source.",
  "inputs": [
